Hi,

I want to create a matrix mix[2,Q] in parameters with an idea that
mix[,1] is 2-simplex, mix[,2] is 2-simplex,...., mix[,Q] is 2-simplex.

To do this, i've written the script below :

parameters{
          simplex[2*Q] all_mix;
}

transformed parameters{
          real mix[2,Q];
          for(q in 1:Q){
                  for(i in 1:2){
                         mix[i,q] <- all_mix[2*(q-1)+p];
                  }
          }
}

My question is i'm not sure to write what i want to obtain.
Indeed, I believe that defining :
           simplex[2*Q] all_mix

implies that the sum of matrix equals 1, but not that each sum of the matrix columns equals 1. Yet, my aim is that each sum of the columns of the matrix equals 1...

Could you help me, please ?
Bests regards

On Thursday, April 2, 2015 at 10:51:56 AM UTC-4, Eric Innocents EBOULET wrote:
> Indeed, I believe that defining :
>            simplex[2*Q] all_mix
>
> implies that the sum of matrix equals 1, but not that each sum of the matrix columns equals 1. Yet, my aim is that each sum of the columns of the matrix equals 1...

It is pretty easy if you generalize your conceptualization of a matrix to be a collection of Q vectors on a J-simplex. So in general you could do

simplex[J] mix[Q];

and access the q-th simplex vector by

mix[q];

But in the case that J = 2, you could also do

parameters {
  vector<lower=0,upper=1>[Q] mix_1;
}
transformed parameters {
  matrix[Q,2] mix;
  mix <- append_col(mix_1, 1 - mix_1);
}

Ben

