Dear Stan community,

I am getting fairly different results for the random effects of my hierarchical models when I estimate these models with Stan in comparison to when I estimate them with R functions such as MCMChregress or lmer.

I have tried my best to find why my specification in Stan yields different results, but with no success. Therefore, I came ask for your help. To make things clearer, I reviewed my code in light of recent discussions I've found here in the list and I also send a chunk of the data (the data can be directly load in R with the "load" R function, which will create the object "db", which stands for database). I also send the R code which contains my call for MCMChregress and lmer, as well as the model and call for Stan. In the end, it also summarizes the sampled posterior mean of the X1 random effects across 2nd level units. Here it goes:

################################
##### beginning of the code ####
################################

load("C:/mypath/db.saved") #data attached in this message

require("lme4"); require("MCMCpack"); require("rstan")

#### lmer model ####
m.lmer <- lmer(y ~ x1 + x2 + (1 + x1 + x2 | L2), data=db, na.action="na.exclude")
View(ranef(m.lmer)$L2)

#### MCMChregress model ####
m.mcmch <- MCMChregress(fixed= y ~ x1 + x2,
 random=~x1+x2, group="L2",
 data=db, burnin=5000, mcmc=5000, thin=1,verbose=1,
 seed=NA, beta.start=0, sigma2.start=1,
 Vb.start=1, mubeta=0, Vbeta=1.0E6,
 r=3, R=diag(c(1,1,1)), nu=0.001, delta=0.001)
m.mcmch.res <- cbind(summary(m.mcmch$mcmc)$statistics,
 summary(m.mcmch$mcmc)$quantile)
View(m.mcmch.res)

#### Stan model ####
require("rstan")

db.stan <- list("y"=db$y,
 "x1"=db$x1,
 "x2"=db$x2,
 "L2"=db$L2,
 "N"=nrow(db),
 "G"=length(unique(db$L2)),
 "W"=diag(c(1,1,1))
)

m.stan <-'
data {
int<lower=1> N;
int<lower=1> G;
real y[N];
real x1[N];
real x2[N];
matrix[3,3] W;
int L2[N];
}

parameters {
real<lower=0> sigma_y;
vector[G] a;
vector[G] b1;
vector[G] b2;
real mu_a;
real mu_b1;
real mu_b2;
real<lower=0> tau_a;
real<lower=0> tau_b1;
real<lower=0> tau_b2;
vector[3] B_raw_temp;
corr_matrix[3] Tau_b_raw;
}

model {
matrix[G,3] B_raw_hat;
matrix[3,3] Sigma_b_raw;
matrix[G,3] B_raw;

mu_a ~ normal(0, 100);
mu_b1 ~ normal(0, 100);
mu_b2 ~ normal(0, 100);
tau_a ~ cauchy(0,2.5);
tau_b1 ~ cauchy(0,2.5);
tau_b2 ~ cauchy(0,2.5);

Tau_b_raw ~ wishart(3, W);
Sigma_b_raw <- inverse(Tau_b_raw);

for (l in 1:G) {
B_raw_hat[l,1] <- mu_a;
B_raw_hat[l,2] <- mu_b1;
B_raw_hat[l,3] <- mu_b2;
B_raw_temp ~ multi_normal(transpose(row(B_raw_hat,l)), Sigma_b_raw);
B_raw[l,1] <- B_raw_temp[1];
B_raw[l,2] <- B_raw_temp[2];
B_raw[l,3] <- B_raw_temp[3];
a[l] ~ normal(B_raw[l,1], tau_a);
b1[l] ~ normal(B_raw[l,2], tau_b1);
b2[l] ~ normal(B_raw[l,3], tau_b2);
}

sigma_y ~ cauchy(0,2.5);

for (n in 1:N)
y[n] ~ normal(a[L2[n]] + b1[L2[n]]*x1[n] + b2[L2[n]]*x2[n], sigma_y);

}
'

fit.stan <- stan(model_code = m.stan,
 data = db.stan,
 iter = 5000,
 chains = 1,
 init="random",
 thin=1)
fit.stan

#### Summary of the sampled mean of x1 random effects ####
 #with lmer:
 summary(as.data.frame(ranef(m.lmer)$L2)$x1)
 #with MCMChregress:
 summary(m.mcmch.res[grep("b.x1.",rownames(m.mcmch.res)),"Mean"])
 #with Stan:
 summary(unlist(lapply(as.data.frame(extract(fit.stan)$b1),mean)))

################################
######## end of the code #######
################################

You all will notice that it is a fairly simplified model, so the debug can be made easier. Still, it is a model with a Wishart prior on the covariance between random effects, trying to make it comparable to how MCMChregress works.

I will be glad to receive any suggestions you might have on why the random effects are so different in my Stan model.

Thanks in advance for your attention and sorry if I am overlooking any obvious detail here.

Fabricio Vasselai
University of São Paulo

>From time to time I need to deal with a model that involves a latent variable that is constrained to lie within an interval. For example, I am currently working with a model that has duration as an independent variable, but the duration is only known roughly -- I have upper and lower bounds on it.  I introduce a latent "duration" variable for each observation, constrained to lie within the bounds for that observation.

The problem is that these models always run excruciatingly slowly in Stan. On this current example it was taking so long that I had to dial down to only 100 iterations total (50 burnin, 50 sampling) just to get some sort of output I could look at to diagnose the problem. Those 100 iterations took 4814 seconds (72 minutes). Taking a look at the sampler parameters, I saw that n_leapfrog__ had an average value of 1454. Ouch!

Thinking about it for a bit, I realized what the problem is. If I understand correctly, HMC and NUTS work best when the curvature of the log probability density is nearly constant, as this means that the same step size is optimal in all parts of the posterior. But the transformation that Stan uses for interval-constrained variables -- inverse logit -- introduces a Jacobian adjustment term whose curvature varies wildly, from -1/2 when x=0 (where x is the unconstrained variable) and falling to 0 as |x| gets large (see attached image). If you have 23000 interval-constrained latent variables, this is a big problem.

The solution is to use the normal CDF to transform from the unconstrained to the constrained variable. Its derivative is the normal density, the log of that is a quadratic function, and the curvature of a quadratic function is constant -- perfect for HMC!

To be specific, I replaced

    parameters {
      vector<lower=0, upper=1>[N] y0;
      ....
    }
    transformed parameters {
      vector[N] y;
      y <- lower_bound + (upper_bound - lower_bound) .* y0;
      ...
    }

with

    parameters {
      vector[N] x;
      ....
    }
    transformed parameters {
      vector[N] y;
      for (i in 1:N)
        y[i] <- lower_bound[i] + (upper_bound[i] - lower_bound[i]) * normal_cdf(x[i], 0, 1);
      ...
    }
    model {
      increment_log_prob(-0.5 * dot_self(x));
      ...
    }

The result: the time for those 100 iterations went down by a factor of 2.9 (!) and the average value for n_leap__ went down by a factor of 2.2, to 655.

This suggests that maybe the normal CDF instead of inverse logit should be the transform that Stan uses for interval-constrained variables.

